 ## Overview
-Two-Stage Inverse Rendering Optimization decouples the recovery of scene geometry from material reflectance and illumination by alternating between an approximate differentiable renderer and a physically-based ray tracer [S1, S2]. It solves the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering by first establishing a robust geometric proxy using silhouette constraints, then refining physically accurate albedo and environment maps while holding geometry fixed [S1, S2]. This approach differs from single-stage radiance field methods (e.g., [NeRF](44-radiance-field-optimization.md), [PhySG](44-radiance-field-optimization.md)) by explicitly modeling triangle meshes or Gaussian splats with PBR materials, enabling direct deployment in graphics engines and superior specular handling [S1].
+Two-Stage Inverse Rendering Optimization decouples the recovery of scene geometry from material reflectance and illumination by alternating between an approximate differentiable renderer and a physically-based ray tracer [S1, S2]. It addresses the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering by first establishing a robust geometric proxy using silhouette constraints, then refining physically accurate albedo and environment maps while holding geometry fixed [S1, S2]. This approach differs from single-stage radiance field methods (e.g., [NeRF](44-radiance-field-optimization.md), [PhySG](44-radiance-field-optimization.md)) by explicitly modeling triangle meshes or Gaussian splats with PBR materials, enabling direct deployment in graphics engines and superior specular handling [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in two distinct phases. The specific variables involved are: $\mu$ (position), $\Sigma$ (covariance), $c$ (color), $\alpha$ (opacity), $n$ (normal), $\rho$ (albedo), $r$ (roughness), and $L_{env}$ (environment-map radiance) [S2].
+The method operates in two distinct phases [S1, S2]. The specific variables involved are: $\mu$ (position), $\Sigma$ (covariance), $c$ (color), $\alpha$ (opacity), $n$ (normal), $\rho$ (albedo), $r$ (roughness), and $L_{env}$ (environment-map radiance) [S2].
 
